To design a highly concurrent, bounded, priority-based queue with per-element Time-To-Live (TTL) in Java, we must balance two opposing ordering constraints: priority ordering (for poll/peek) and expiration ordering (for eviction and TTL cleanup), all while ensuring thread safety and a bounded capacity \(M\).
Here is a comprehensive architecture, design trade-offs, and an implementation strategy optimized for high throughput.

1. Data-Structure Design & Thread Safety

To achieve thread safety without heavy global locking, we can combine a PriorityBlockingQueue (for priority-based retrieval) with a ConcurrentSkipListSet or a secondary concurrent map to track expiration. However, standard concurrent collections don't easily support atomic "lowest-priority eviction" when full.
The Optimal Choice: A combination of StampedLock (or standard explicit ReentrantLock with multiple conditions) and two specialized sorting structures wrapped inside a single monitor:
• Priority Heap (PriorityQueue): Sorted by priority (descending) to serve poll() and peek() in \(O(\log M)\).
• Expiration Tree (TreeSet or ConcurrentSkipListSet): Sorted by expiryTime (ascending) to identify the next element to expire in \(O(1)\) to \(O(\log M)\).
To maximize concurrency and avoid global lock contention, we can use a ReentrantLock protecting the internal state, paired with a dedicated background ScheduledExecutorService or DelayQueue architecture for aggressive cleanup.

2. Eviction & Expiration Handling Strategies

• Eviction Strategy (Queue Full): When offer() is called and size == M, we check the expiration tree.
	1. If there are already expired elements, we evict the most expired one.
	2. If no elements are expired, we must evict the lowest-priority element currently in the queue to make room for the new one. To do this efficiently, the priority heap must allow us to inspect/remove the tail, or we maintain a dual-sorted TreeSet (one sorted by priority, one by expiry).
• Expiration Handling (Lazy + Active Hybrid):
	• Lazy Removal: Every time poll(), peek(), or size() is invoked, the queue proactively checks its head. If the highest-priority element is expired, it is purged, and the check loops until a valid element or an empty queue is found.
	• Active Background Cleaning: To prevent memory leaks when the queue is idle, a single background wheel or ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor tracks the earliest expiration time via the expiration tree and proactively purges dead nodes.

3. Detailed Complexity & Trade-offs

Strategy	offer()	poll() / peek()	Space	Trade-offs
Dual TreeSet + Lock (Chosen)	\(O(\log M)\)	\(O(\log M)\)	\(O(M)\)	Pros: Predictable \(O(\log M)\) bounds for all operations, precise lowest-priority eviction. 
Cons: Coarse-grained locking reduces raw concurrent throughput.
DelayQueue + PriorityQueue	\(O(M)\) eviction	\(O(\log M)\)	\(O(M)\)	Pros: Standard library parts. 
Cons: Evicting the lowest priority element requires scanning the entire DelayQueue (\(O(M)\) linear scan).
ConcurrentSkipListMap	\(O(\log M)\) amortized	\(O(\log M)\) amortized	\(O(M)\)	Pros: Lock-free reads/writes. 
Cons: Hard to atomically balance dual indexes (priority vs expiry) without race conditions.

4. Java Implementation Sketch

The following implementation uses a unified thread-safe wrapper around two structural indices. To break ties uniquely in the trees (since TreeSet collapses elements if compareTo returns 0), a unique sequenceNumber is attached to every element.
java
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.TreeSet;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

public class BoundedPriorityTTLQueue<E> {

    // Helper class to encapsulate the element wrapper
    private static class Node<T> {
        final T element;
        final int priority;
        final long expiryTime;
        final long sequenceId;

        Node(T element, int priority, long ttlMillis, long sequenceId) {
            this.element = element;
            this.priority = priority;
            this.expiryTime = System.currentTimeMillis() + ttlMillis;
            this.sequenceId = sequenceId;
        }

        boolean isExpired() {
            return System.currentTimeMillis() > this.expiryTime;
        }
    }

    private final int maxSize;
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final AtomicLong seqCounter = new AtomicLong(0);

    // Index 1: Sorted by Priority (Highest first). Tie-break with oldest sequence ID.
    private final TreeSet<Node<E>> priorityIndex = new TreeSet<>((a, b) -> {
        if (a.priority != b.priority) {
            return Integer.compare(b.priority, a.priority); // Descending
        }
        return Long.compare(a.sequenceId, b.sequenceId);
    });

    // Index 2: Sorted by Expiry Time (Earliest first). Tie-break with sequence ID.
    private final TreeSet<Node<E>> expiryIndex = new TreeSet<>((a, b) -> {
        if (a.expiryTime != b.expiryTime) {
            return Long.compare(a.expiryTime, b.expiryTime); // Ascending
        }
        return Long.compare(a.sequenceId, b.sequenceId);
    });

    public BoundedPriorityTTLQueue(int maxSize) {
        this.maxSize = maxSize;
    }

    public void offer(E element, int priority, long ttlMillis) {
        if (element == null) throw new NullPointerException();

        lock.lock();
        try {
            // Clean up any naturally expired elements first to free up space
            cleanExpired();

            Node<E> newNode = new Node<>(element, priority, ttlMillis, seqCounter.getAndIncrement());

            if (priorityIndex.size() >= maxSize) {
                // Queue is completely full with active elements. Evict lowest priority.
                Node<E> lowestPriorityNode = priorityIndex.last();

                // Optional Optimization: If the new element has lower priority than the lowest, drop it immediately
                if (newNode.priority <= lowestPriorityNode.priority) {
                    return; // New node cannot displace existing ones
                }

                // Evict the lowest priority node
                priorityIndex.remove(lowestPriorityNode);
                expiryIndex.remove(lowestPriorityNode);
            }

            // Insert new node into both tracking indexes
            priorityIndex.add(newNode);
            expiryIndex.add(newNode);
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public E poll() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            cleanExpired();
            if (priorityIndex.isEmpty()) {
                return null;
            }
            Node<E> highest = priorityIndex.pollFirst();
            expiryIndex.remove(highest);
            return highest.element;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public E peek() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            cleanExpired();
            if (priorityIndex.isEmpty()) {
                return null;
            }
            return priorityIndex.first().element;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public int size() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            cleanExpired();
            return priorityIndex.size();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    /**
     * Internal helper to lazily evict all expired elements from both indexes.
     * Runs in O(K log M) where K is the number of expired elements.
     */
    private void cleanExpired() {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
        while (!expiryIndex.isEmpty()) {
            Node<E> earliest = expiryIndex.first();
            if (earliest.expiryTime <= now) {
                expiryIndex.pollFirst();
                priorityIndex.remove(earliest);
            } else {
                break; // Earliest item hasn't expired yet, so none after it have either
            }
        }
    }
}
